# Q2 — Do server logs reach container stdout?
Date: 2026-10-13. Container agency-spike-q (base image, dummy key).

## Commands + trimmed output

$ docker logs agency-spike-q | head -n 5
server listening on http://0.0.0.0:4096

$ docker exec agency-spike-q find /home/agent/.local /home/agent/.cache -name '*.log'
/home/agent/.local/share/opencode/log/opencode.log

$ docker exec agency-spike-q tail -n 3 /home/agent/.local/share/opencode/log/opencode.log
timestamp=... level=INFO ... message="watcher started" path=/work type=entries ...
timestamp=... level=INFO ... message=event ... event.type=model.updated ...
timestamp=... level=INFO ... message=event ... event.type=agent.updated ...

Source: review/opencode/packages/cli/src/commands/commands.ts
  PrintLogs = GlobalFlag.Setting("print-logs")({
    flag: Flag.Boolean("print-logs")  // "Print logs to stderr (server logs require --standalone)"

$ docker exec agency-spike-q opencode serve --help | grep print-logs
  --print-logs   Print logs to stderr (server logs require --standalone)

## Observation
Yes. `opencode serve` logs to container stdout without any flag
(`docker logs` shows startup + request lines), and the structured log is
also tailed at ~/.local/share/opencode/log/opencode.log. --print-logs
exists but targets stderr for standalone runs; serve needs no fallback.

## Verdict: yes
